          "derivation": "Proto-Germanic *laikaną 'to jump, play, move, swing about' — the o-grade *loyg-e-ti of the root, with the regular Grimm shift *g > k. It is a strong class-7 verb with reduplicated preterite (*lelaik), whence Gothic laikan (pret. lailaik) 'to leap, dance', Old Norse leika 'to play', Old English lācan 'to move quickly, play, fight', Old High German leih 'melody, lay' (noun *laikaz > Gothic laiks 'dance', ON leikr 'game'). Nourai's citation form 'laik' is the bare Germanic stem rather than a full infinitive, but the form and gloss are right.",
